  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDry conditions continue through the remainder of the afternoon with\ntemperatures comfortably rising into the 60s. A surface low pressure\nis approaching the region, and it will provide the next round of rain\nlater this evening. \n\nRegarding the severe threat, a Marginal risk remains in place for\nportions of southeast Indiana and northern Kentucky. The best\nsurface-based CAPE envelope develops within the warm sector over \nsouthern Indiana and far western Kentucky. This is where storms will \ndevelop later this afternoon before moving east toward the local area\nbetween 6-7pm. By then, the surface instability will wain, \nsuggesting a decreasing severe threat as storms enter the local area.\nDespite this, the additional forcing provided by the low pressure on\nthe northern periphery of the MUCAPE, along with the steep mid-level\nlapse rates, may be enough to support some hail within the strongest\nstorms as they move in. The damaging wind and tornado threat are \nlikely highest west of the area where the surface- based CAPE \nresides. Otherwise, sub-severe thunderstorms linger into the first\npart of the evening before weakening. \n\nAs the low pressure pulls east of the area into Thursday morning, \nnortherly winds will accelerate in response to the tightened pressure\ngradient. Wind gusts between 25 to 30 mph are expected with a few\nisolated wind gusts approaching 35 degrees. Breezy conditions remain\nin place through much of the day Thursday. Temperatures are much\nbelow normal the next few days, with high temperatures stuck in the\n40s both Friday and Saturday. \n\nEven though the surface low pressure center shifts off to the east,\nthe combination of moist northerly flow and upper level disturbances\nwithin the trough will continue to support clouds and shower\npotential Thursday and Friday. Some of these clouds and showers may\nlinger into Saturday. Much of the precipitation is expected to be\nliquid, but in stronger/deeper showers, forecast soundings indicate\nsaturation within the DGZ, supporting snowflake generation aloft.\nThis means some locations across the area likely see their first\nflakes of the season. \n\nMoving into next week, longer range guidance is struggling with a\nweak trough ejecting eastward from the Plains. This may cause a\nwrinkle in the broader expectation of dry conditions and slowly\nmoderating temperatures that are currently forecast.",
